title The archive-fold tool could not build, and its own selftest had drifted underneath
prompt No new user turn this lap; the baton's rite and incense-inner.md's own "next" section named the owed work: the archive fold that gives fold_shelf_link a clean place to land, and the stale rishi rebuild.
think Round-opened, read the claim board (clear), read HEAD once (515684963b).
think Read incense-inner.md's next section: two items owed -- rebuild rishi (stale by ~523,141s), and run the archive fold so fold_shelf_link's seventeen depth-lost links get repointed.
think Rebuilt rye and rishi first; both went from stale to fresh.
think Ran session_logs_archive_preview.rish to open the archive fold door -- it refused at the build step: a Zig compile error, redeclaration of local constant is_kyri.
think Traced it to commit 2ece48742, the 20260921 bron-to-kyri breach, which turned two adjacent const is_bron / const is_kyri lines into two identical const is_kyri lines via a blind rename.
think Removed the duplicate. Build succeeded. Ran the witness's selftest -- it failed a different assertion, SelftestShelfDepth, expecting a two-up ORGANIZING.md link where the transform actually produces four-up.
think git log -S traced the fixture's zeta row: it originally read one-up (../ORGANIZING.md), correct for the selftest's two-up assert on a one-more-../ transform, then commit cc9053b03 ("ten stale citations repaired") widened it to three-up so the link would resolve from the fixture's own real directory -- correct for a human reader, silently wrong for the selftest's fixed string.
think Rather than reverting the fixture (which would re-break real link resolution and get flagged by the backtick_path/comment_path family again), updated the assert to expect the four-up answer the widened link now produces, with a comment naming why.
think Witness GREEN. Ran fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh for real -- it closed all seventeen depth-lost links across ten dated archive shelves (fold_depth_lost 17 to 0).
think Booked both findings as REDS rows. The REDS pin was already near its 65536-byte bound; my rows plus overhead pushed it over, so I folded the large CLOSED row %767 (receipt-contract borrowed-ceiling finding) to its own archive shelf using reds_fold.sh, per the established pattern (row %817's own prior fold).
think Ran reds_fold_witness.rish to confirm the fold -- its own control script failed at setup: build_pen() wrote a fixture shelf into construction/archive/REDS/ without ever making that subdirectory, so every leg in the control ran unproven. Confirmed pre-existing via git stash (same failure on HEAD before my edits).
think Fixed both mkdir -p call sites in reds_fold_control.sh to make the REDS subdirectory. 62 legs green, witness GREEN. Booked as a third REDS row, still under the pin bound.
think Shelved my own prior live account on ITINERARY.md's card (the "cold roster's 42 reds" account) per the-writer-sheds, and wrote a fresh short account naming what this lap closed against it.
think Updated incense-inner.md's next section with the day's account, and construction/ITINERARY.md's Git nib to this commit's parent (515684963b).
think Graded incense-inner.md with qa_report_card.sh: A, 90, well above the B floor.
obs Two genuinely independent, pre-existing reds were found in the course of doing owed maintenance work, both from search-and-replace or citation-repair passes that were individually correct and collectively silent about a downstream assumption they broke. Neither was caused by this lap; both are now closed on witnesses.
obs The REDS pin bound is tight enough (headroom under 3KB before my edits) that any two or three new rows require a fold in the same lap -- worth naming as a pattern rather than a surprise each time.
loom witness=session_logs_archive_witness wall_ms=unmeasured verdict=green
loom witness=reds_fold_witness legs=62 fail=0 verdict=green
rota lap 6, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; the reading surfaced that a tool's absence (rather than its failure) is the hardest kind of red to notice, since nothing announces a silence.
